// DF-151i: the tuple transfer refusal recommends `.copy()`, and `.copy()` now
// exists to be recommended.
//
// This pins the half of the contradiction that was always RIGHT. An ExplicitCopy
// tuple refuses a plain transfer and tells the author to write `.copy()` — good
// advice, except that until this finding was fixed the very next line produced a
// second error saying that same tuple was "not Copy", so following the hint led
// straight into a refusal. The tuple was the one design-139 wrapper the rule
// named that never got the method, which made an ExplicitCopy tuple move-only in
// practice: `move` retires the binding, so a program that needed the original
// AND a duplicate had no spelling at all.
//
// The two files are a pair and are meant to be read together. This one says the
// compiler still recommends `.copy()` here; `df151i_tuple_copy.saw` says the
// recommendation compiles, deep-copies each element at its own tier, and
// balances its drops. A change that silences either one without the other has
// re-opened the contradiction.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot copy value of type
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: which implements ExplicitCopy
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: use .copy() for an explicit deep copy

func main() {
    var v: Vector<Int> = Vector<Int>()
    v.push(1)
    let t = (move v, 5)
    let u = t
    print("{u.1}")
}
